  "sgg_form_id": "sf424",
  "description": "The standard cover page form used for most federal grant applications. Collects applicant organization information, project details, estimated funding, and authorized representative certification.",
  "notes": [
    "The answer follows the SF-424 family's shared structure: opportunity, application, applicant, project, contacts, budget, and certification. The sections on screen keep the paper form's order, so the applicant's district shows under Project Information and the signature and date under Authorized Representative. The contact does not ask for an address. Department and division keep the 30-character source limit.",
    "Eight opportunity or organization values are pre-populated. Date Received, signature, and signing date belong to submission. The funding total is read-only; SGG sums the six inputs with the same sum_monetary rule as the existing form.",
    "Four attachment purposes remain distinct. The older XML transform format cannot write the three nested single attachments. The structured mapping records their source elements for integration.",
    "The ordered applicant-type list represents the three DAT and XML source slots. The XML mapping writes each item to ApplicantTypeCode1–3. DAT 09-1/09-2 forbid selecting the same code twice, so the form rejects duplicate applicant types.",
    "The State/Province choice uses the common address. The shared ZIP+4 question requires all nine digits for a US address, as the DAT does.",
    "The State Review choices use the original XSD casing. Existing SGG lowercases state in two saved strings and normalizes them during XML generation."
  ],
  "risks": [
    "Amounts use the shared budget questions, which follow the XSD: no negative amounts, at most 12 digits before the decimal point (13 for the total), and exactly two after it when present. The existing form accepted a leading minus and up to 14 characters.",
    "The definition declares the DAT 12 not-before rule on the project end date, but the SGG emitter does not emit it yet, so the API accepts an end date before the start date."
  ],
